<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Numerical Integration: Midpoint, Trapezoid, and Simpson's Rules.</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Use \(n={@n@}\) to approximate the value of \( \displaystyle \int_{@a@}^{@b@} {@f(x)@}\,dx \) to 2 decimal places;</p>
<p>Give your answer in 4 decimal places.</p>
<p>What is the approximate area by:</p>
<p> Midpoint rule \(M_{@n@}=\)[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>What is the error in approximating the value using midpoint rule \(=\)[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]] </p>
<p>Trapezoid  rule\(T_{@n@}=\)[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>
<p>What is the error in approximating the value using Trapezoid rule \(=\)[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]] </p></p>
<p>Simpson's  rule \(S_{@n@}=\)[[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]][[feedback:prt5]]</p>
<p>What is the error in approximating the value using Simpson's rule \(=\)[[input:ans6]] [[validation:ans6]] [[feedback:prt6]] </p></p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked solution</b></p>
<p>Here is a sketch of the graph corresponding to \(f(x)={@f(x)@}\).</p>
<p>[[jsxgraph]]
  var board = JXG.JSXGraph.initBoard(divid, { 
            boundingbox: [-2, 7, 9, -7],
            keepaspectratio: false,
            showCopyright: false,
            axis: true,
            showNavigation: false,
   
});
         var graph1 = board.create('functiongraph', ['{#f(x)#}', {#-10#}, {#10#}],{name:'{#f(x)#}', 
            withLabel:true}); 

[[/jsxgraph]]</p>

<p>Now, approximating the definite integral using:</p>
<p>a) Midpoint rule</p>
<p>We need to divide the interval \([{@a@},{@b@}]\) into \({@n@}\) subintervals each with width \(\Delta x\)</p>
<p>\(\Delta x= \frac{{@b@}-{@a@}}{@n@}={@delta_x@}\)</p>
<p>The values of the midpoints for each of the subintervals are then,
</p>
<p>\({@all_midpoints@}\)</p>
<p>Using the midpoint rule to approximate the value of the integral all we need to do is plug into the formula
</p>
<p>\(\Delta x=\frac{b-a}{n}\) if [a,b]is divided into \(n\) subintervals, each length of \(\Delta x\) and \(m_{i}\) is the midpoint of the \(i^{th}\) interval.</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle \int_{@a@}^{@b@} {@f(x)@}\,dx \approx M_{n}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}f(m_{i})\Delta x\)</p>
<p>Therefore,</p>
<p>
    \(
    \begin{align}
    \displaystyle \int_{@a@}^{@b@} {@f(x)@}\,dx &amp; \approx {@ta_1@}\\
    \end{align}
    \)</p>
<p>To find the error in approximating the value of the Integral using Midpoint rule, we substract the approximation from the actual value of the Integral</p>
<p>\({@ta@}-{@ta_1@}={@m_error@}\)</p>
<hr>


<p>b) Trapezoid Rule</p>
<p>We know that the width of each subinterval is \( \Delta x={@delta_x@}\)
</p>
<p>\(\Delta x=\frac{b-a}{n}\) if \([a,b]\) is divided into \(n\) subintervals of each of length \(\Delta x\),with endpoints \(x_{0}\) to \(x_{n}\)</p>
<p>Therefore, the endpoints are:</p>
<p>\( {@all_endpoints@} \)</p>
<p>Using trapezoid rule to approximate the value of the integral,
</p>
<p>\(T_{n}=\frac{\Delta x}{2}(f(x_{0})+2f(x_{1})+2f(x_2)+ \cdots 2f(x_{n-1})+f(x_n))\)</p>
<p>Therefore,</p>
<p>
    \(
    \begin{align}
    \displaystyle \int_{@a@}^{@b@} {@f(x)@}\,dx &amp; \approx {@ta_2@}\\
    \end{align}
    \)
</p>
<p>To find the error in approximating the value of the Integral using Trapezoid rule, we subtract the approximation from the actual value of the Integral</p>
<p>\({@ta@}-{@ta_2@}={@t_error@}\)</p>

<hr>

<p>c) Simpson's rule</p>
<p>We know that the width of each subinterval is \( \Delta x = {@delta_x@} \)
    and for reference purposes the sketch of the graph along with the endpoints of each subinterval marked by the dots is shown above.</p>
<p>The endpoints are:</p>
<p>\( {@all_endpoints@} \)</p>
<p>Applying simpson’s rule,</p>
<p>\(S_n=\frac{\Delta x}{2}(f(x_{0})+4f(x_1)+2f(x_2)+4f(x_3)+2f(x_{4})+ \cdots + 2f(x_{n-2})+4f(x_n-1)+f(x_{n}))\)</p>
<p>Therefore,</p>
<p>
    \(
    \begin{align}

    \displaystyle \int_{@a@}^{@b@} {@f(x)@}\,dx &amp; \approx {@ta_3@}\\
    \end{align}
    \)
</p>
<p>To find the error in approximating the value of the Integral using Simpson's  rule, we subtract the approximation from the actual value of the Integral</p>
<p>\({@ta@}-{@ta_3@}={@s_error@}\)</p>]]></text>
